<header><p class="st-kicker">theocracy · Arabia and the Gulf</p><h1 class="st-title">Islamic state of Medina</h1><p class="st-meta">622 CE – 632 CE · capital Medina</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The first Muslim community-state, founded by Muhammad at Medina in 622, which united most of Arabia within a decade.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 622 Muhammad and his followers left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Yathrib</a>, later called Medina, at the invitation of its feuding Arab tribes. This migration, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hijra">Hijra</a>, became the start of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamic-calendar">Islamic calendar</a>. The document known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-medina">Constitution of Medina</a> bound the emigrants, the local Muslims and the Jewish tribes into a single community, the umma.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Muhammad acted as prophet, judge and military leader. Revelations of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> in this period set out rules on prayer, fasting, alms, marriage and inheritance. The community fought <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/pre-islamic-mecca">Pre-Islamic Mecca</a> at Badr (624), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-uhud">Uhud</a> (625) and the Trench (627), and expelled or defeated the Jewish tribes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-hudaybiyyah">Treaty of Hudaybiyya</a> in 628 brought a truce.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> surrendered in 630, and delegations from across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> submitted in the following two years. When Muhammad died in 632, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-bakr-al-siddiq">Abu Bakr</a> was chosen as his successor, founding the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> remained its capital and is the second holiest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="theocracy, 622 CE – 632 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M30 82 V46 Q50 12 70 46 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 30 V82 M40 58 H60"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">theocracy</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Arabia and the Gulf</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:72.08%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>622 CE – 632 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>theocracy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Medina</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Muhammad</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ummah of Medina, Medina under Muhammad</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=627&amp;focus=islamic-state-of-medina" aria-label="Islamic state of Medina on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5733 1277 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Islamic state of Medina lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6053 1533L6070 1642M6053 1533L6053 1533"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6070 1642h0M6053 1533h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6053 1533h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6053 1533h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Medina. <a href="/atlas?year=627&amp;focus=islamic-state-of-medina">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
